.\" elc.1 — manual page for elc.
.TH ELC 1 "2026-08-20" "elocker" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
elc \- per-function code metrics and whole-project architecture analysis
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B elc
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.IR TARGET ...
.br
.B elc
.B \-\-from\-xml
.I FILE
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.br
.B elc
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B elc
calculates code metrics and performs architecture analysis per function. It calculates metrics based on 
a codebase's syntax tree instead of textual matching.
The definitions are consistent across all supported languages.

.SS Effective lines of code
.B ELOC
is the count of lines carrying an executable statement.
This includes an assignment, operation, control-flow construct, call, return, or exception handling.
Declarations that initialize nothing, preprocessor directives, comments, blank lines, and lines containing only a brace do not count as ELOC.

Because ELOC counts statements instead of physical lines:
.TP
.B Layout does not change the ELOC count.
A single statement spread across four lines counts as one ELOC.
Two statements written on one line count as two ELOC.
.TP
.B A nested function's work belongs to it.
Where a language allows a named function inside another,
.B elc
reports both. Each statement is attributed to the innermost function containing it.
.PP
Comments are identified from the syntax tree, never by matching text. A
comment opener inside a string literal opens nothing, a quote inside a comment
opens no string, and a comment sharing a line with code does not stop that
line counting.
.SS Cyclomatic complexity
A function's cyclomatic complexity is
.B one plus
the number of decision points in it: the number of paths through it. A
function that never branches is 1.
.PP
A decision point is a place execution can go more than one way — an
.BR if ,
a loop, a
.B case
label, a conditional expression, and each short-circuiting
.B &&
or
.BR || ,
since each of those is a second place the condition can be decided. Three
things that look like decisions are not: a bare
.B else
adds no path the
.B if
did not already count, a
.B default
label is where control goes when no branch was taken, and a
.B goto
moves control without choosing.
.PP
As with effective lines of code, a nested named function owns its own
decision points and the function enclosing it gains none of them. A decision
point inside an
.I anonymous
callable — a lambda or closure, which is not reported as a function in its own
right — is attributed to the nearest enclosing named function.
.SS The complexity threshold
.B elc
lists, for each file, the functions whose complexity is at or above a
threshold, set with
.B \-\-complexity\-threshold
and defaulting to 15.
.PP
The threshold changes
.I what is listed
and nothing else. It does not change a total, a callout, or — most
importantly — the exit status. Findings are data; deciding what a number
warrants is the caller's. A build that should fail on a breach acts on the
report rather than expecting
.B elc
to exit non-zero.
.SS The dependence graph
.B elc
resolves the calls between the functions it finds into one project-wide
directed graph, the
.BR "System Dependence Graph" .
It is built from the same single parse that produced the metrics; no source
file is re-read to resolve a cross-file reference.
.PP
Nodes are functions. A call edge joins a caller to a callee across file
boundaries, and repeated calls between one pair collapse to a single edge
carrying a call-site count \(em which is what makes fan-out the number of
.I distinct
subroutines a function invokes. A global edge joins a function that writes a
global to every function that reads it, so coupling through shared state is
represented alongside coupling through calls. The two kinds are never merged.
.PP
A call whose target is not defined within the analysis target \(em a library
call, a system call, an indirect call through a pointer \(em is
.IR unresolved .
It is counted and reported in the project summary, and it is neither an error
nor a defect: a program that calls
.BR printf (3)
has unresolved calls by definition. The count states how complete the graph
is, so that a fan-out figure can be read in the knowledge of how much left
the target. No destination is ever guessed at; an edge that does not exist
would make a later dead-code proof unsound.
.PP
Calls are resolved by name. A name defined more than once resolves to its
first definition in sorted file order, with a diagnostic naming it, and a
method call resolves on the method name rather than on the receiver's type.
.B elc
does not correct for either, because the correction would place one language's
semantics in the executable.
.SS Call-tree analyses
For every function,
.B elc
reports its
.B fan-out
\(em the number of
.I distinct
subroutines it invokes. A function calling one helper twice is coupled to one
thing and reports 1. The measurement itself carries no judgement; the bands
that turn it into a finding are the published thresholds tabulated under
.B "Findings, severity, and attribution"
below, and the measurement is reported whether or not it crosses one.
.PP
.B Recursion
is detected statically, direct and mutual alike, in accordance with MISRA C
Rule 17.2 \(em under which recursion is forbidden because it makes worst-case
stack depth unpredictable. The functions of each recursive group are listed as
a
.IR set ,
not as a path: the analysis establishes that they can reach one another, and
does not produce a particular cycle through them.
.PP
The
.B deepest call chain
is reported in full \(em the ordered sequence of functions from entry point to
deepest leaf \(em rather than only its length, since the length alone does not
say which path to shorten. It requires entry points declared with
.BR \-\-entry .
.PP
.B Coupling
is reported per
.IR component ,
and a component is a source file. Afferent coupling
.RB ( Ca )
is the number of components depending on it; efferent coupling
.RB ( Ce )
is the number it depends upon. A dependency exists where any function in one
file calls any function in another, or writes a global another reads \(em and
it is counted once however many times it occurs, so a file calling another in
forty places depends on it once.
.PP
.B Instability
is
.IR "Ce / (Ce + Ca)" ,
after Martin: approaching 0 it denotes maximum stability \(em widely depended
upon, depending on little, and dangerous to change \(em and approaching 1,
maximum instability. Where both couplings are zero the metric is reported
.B undefined
rather than computed. A file nothing depends on that depends on nothing is
entirely ordinary, and reporting 0 there would claim maximum stability for a
component with no relationships at all.
.PP
.B Component dependency cycles
are the mutually dependent groups of the component projection, each reported
with its whole membership and with a concrete loop through it \(em the group
is what must be broken up, and the loop says which edge to cut. This is a
different finding from the recursion above, and the difference is the unit:
two mutually recursive functions in one file are a recursion finding and no
cycle at all, because a file does not depend on itself. The same pair split
across two files is legitimately both.
.PP
.B Layering
is validated only where strata are declared. Two findings come out of one
comparison of the caller's layer against the callee's, and they are
.I orthogonal:
a call bypassing an intervening layer is
.BR skip-level ,
and a call running against the declared direction is
.BR inverted .
A call descending two layers skips without inverting; a call ascending one
inverts without skipping; a call ascending two does both, and is reported
twice because both statements are true of it and each has its own remedy. A
file matching no declaration lies outside the partition, and a call touching it
is neither. Only calls are considered \(em a global two layers share is a
different fact with its own analyses.
.PP
.B Unreachable functions
are found by traversing the call graph forward from a root set, and reporting
everything the traversal never visits. The root set is the declared entry
points
.I together with
every function whose address is taken. That second half is what makes the claim
sound rather than merely plausible: a handler installed in an interrupt vector
or stored in a callback table has no resolved caller anywhere in the graph, and
omitting it would report live code as provably dead. The asymmetry is
deliberate \(em an extra root can only shrink the unreachable set, whereas a
missing one produces a false claim of death.
.PP
Because the answer comes from traversal alone and from no textual heuristic, a
clique of unused functions that call one another is correctly reported: each
has a caller, so a "nothing calls this" rule finds neither, and no path reaches
the pair from any root. A global object every one of whose accessing functions
is unreachable is reported unreachable too. Where no entry points are declared,
nothing at all is reported unreachable.
.PP
One caveat belongs with that list, and it runs against the direction
everything above errs in. Calls resolve by name, so where two files each
define a
.B static
helper of the same name, every call resolves to the first and the second has
no incoming edge \(em and is reported unreachable when it is not. The same
artefact moves coupling from one component to another and can close a
dependency cycle that does not exist, which is the more expensive form of the
mistake. The duplicate definition is diagnosed on standard error where it
occurs, so read that before acting on either list.
.PP
The same artefact reaches the annotated call tree, where it is easier to
circulate and harder to caveat: a function wrongly reported unreachable is
drawn dashed, an invented dependency cycle colours two clusters, and a
recursive cycle is drawn on every function sharing a member's name. Read the
stderr diagnostics alongside any drawing taken from a tree with duplicate
.B static
names in it.
.PP
.B Global state
is reported for every object: the set of functions that write it and the set
that read it. Two arrangements are flagged, both under MISRA C Rule 8.9. An
object whose identifier appears in a single function belongs at block scope. An
object shared between functions lying in
.I disconnected
regions of the call graph is a hidden channel \(em temporal coupling in which
execution order silently governs whether the system works, with nothing in
either region saying so. Sharing within one call-connected region is ordinary
and is reported as a measurement without a finding.
.PP
.B Dead code within a function
is a different question answered by different means, and neither analysis
subsumes the other: a function reached from an entry point may contain
statements that cannot execute, and an unreachable function may contain none.
Two classes are detected from the syntax tree alone \(em statements following a
.BR return ", " break ", " continue
or unconditional transfer, up to the first construct that can be entered
without falling into it; and the branch a
.I literally written
condition excludes, such as the body of
.B if (0)
or the
.B else
of
.BR "if (1)" .
.PP
.B elc
performs no data-flow analysis, no constant propagation, and no evaluation of
expressions here. A branch guarded by a variable is never claimed dead however
clearly a reader can infer its value: a missed statement costs a cleanup
opportunity, while a false claim invites deleting code that runs. Support is
per language and its absence is stated \(em a language module supplying no
dead-code query has its files analysed for everything else, and the report says
the analysis was
.I not performed
for that language rather than that none was found.
.PP
Two properties of the depth are stated in its heading rather than left to be
assumed. It is a
.B lower bound:
a chain continuing through a call that could not be resolved is not followed,
so the unresolved-call count is presented beside it and a depth measured
against many unresolved calls should not be relied upon. And where the call
graph is
.BR recursive ,
no depth is reported at all: the longest path has no finite answer, so
.B elc
reports the cycle in place of a number rather than a misleading finite value.
.SS Language support
.B elc
determines each file's language from its extension, and loads the parser and
queries for that language from the runtime directory at first use.

It supports these out of the box:
.TS
tab(;);
l l.
C;\fB.c .h\fR
C++;\fB.cc .cpp .cxx .hh .hpp .hxx\fR
Rust;\fB.rs\fR
Python;\fB.py .pyi\fR
.TE
.PP
.B .h
maps to C rather than to C++, because a header shared by both is far more
often C. A project that knows otherwise edits
.I runtime/extensions.map
and rebuilds nothing.
.PP
Files of different languages in one target are analysed in a single
invocation, and each language's contribution appears separately in the
report.
.PP
A file whose extension maps to no available language is
.I skipped
rather than failed: it is named on standard error, listed in the report's
skipped-file section, and does not affect the exit status. A language whose
module is unusable — absent, exposing no entry point, or carrying a query
file that will not compile — is reported once, excluded, and the run
continues over the remaining languages. Only a runtime directory that yields
no language at all is fatal.
.SS Discovery
A target that is a regular file is analysed directly, with no traversal. A
target that is a directory is discovered by one of two routes.
.PP
When the target directory is tracked by a Git repository,
.B elc
analyses the files tracked at
.BR HEAD .
A file that is not committed is therefore not analysed, whether it was never
added or is merely a change not yet committed; the analysis is of the tree at
.BR HEAD ,
not of the working directory. Files are excluded because git does not track
them and not because
.I .gitignore
was consulted \(em
.B elc
never reads that file \(em and a tracked blob is excluded when git reports its
content as binary, whatever its name. Naming a subdirectory analyses that
subdirectory alone, not the repository around it.
.PP
When the target is in no repository, or in one that does not track it \(em a
.IR .gitignore d
build directory, or a directory under a version-controlled home directory \(em
the directory is walked recursively instead.
.PP
By either route
.B elc
excludes hidden files and hidden directories, and files whose extension
appears in
.IR runtime/binary.exts .
The route applied to each directory target is named in the report, so that a
result which is unexpectedly empty, or unexpectedly larger than the target,
can be diagnosed rather than guessed at.
.PP
A symbolic link encountered during the walk is not followed, so a directory
reached through a link is never descended into and a self-referential link
cannot produce an unbounded traversal. A symbolic link named
.I directly
as a target is resolved and analysed, since naming it identifies its referent.
.PP
Every target is validated before any of them is walked, so an invalid target
is reported whatever else was named and no report is produced that covers
fewer targets than were given. Each discovered file is reduced to its
canonical absolute path and analysed exactly once, however many targets reach
it.
.SH FORMATS
A format is named in one of two ways, and which is available depends on where
the report is going. Where an
.B \-\-output
file is named, the extension of that filename determines the format and no
option is required to repeat what the filename has already said. Standard
output has no filename and therefore no extension, so a report written there
takes its format from
.B \-\-format
alone, defaulting to
.BR table .
.PP
.RS
.TP
.B \&.txt
the aligned table, as
.BR "\-f table" .
.TP
.B \&.md
GitHub\-Flavored Markdown, as
.BR "\-f md" .
.TP
.B \&.csv
RFC 4180 CSV, as
.BR "\-f csv" .
.TP
.B \&.xml
the complete record, as
.BR "\-f xml" .
.RE
.PP
An output filename carrying any other extension, or none at all, is a usage
error naming the extension found and listing those that are recognized.
Guessing would write one format under a name promising another, and
defaulting silently to the table would produce a
.B report.json
holding no JSON. Where both an output filename and
.B \-\-format
are given they are two statements of one fact: agreement is accepted, and
disagreement is a usage error naming both rather than a silent preference for
either.
.PP
The extension governs the format alone. The companion artefacts substitute
their own extension on the same path, so an
.B \-\-output
of
.B report.md
still yields
.B report.dot
and
.BR report.graphml .
.TP
.B table
An aligned table for reading. The default.
.TP
.B md
The same report as GitHub-Flavored Markdown. The two present the same tiers
in the same order and differ only in decoration, because they are one
traversal of one model.
.TP
.B csv
One record per function, RFC 4180, with the complete dataset: the complexity
threshold does not filter it. The architectural findings are absent, not
omitted — they are not expressible as a single flat record set. Every field
that contains a comma, a quotation mark, or a line break is quoted.
.TP
.B xml
The complete record of a run, carrying every element any report may present
and a format-version identifier. It is what
.B \-\-from\-xml
reads back.
.SH THE SAVED RECORD
.B elc \-f xml
writes a record that is sufficient on its own to produce a report later,
without the source it describes:
.PP
.EX
elc \-f xml \-o build/metrics.xml src/
elc \-\-from\-xml build/metrics.xml > report.md
.EE
.PP
The regenerated Markdown is
.I byte-identical
to the Markdown a direct analysis would have produced at the same threshold.
It is identical rather than merely equivalent because the record carries the
measurements and nothing derived from them: the totals, the callouts, the
ordering, and the threshold listing are all computed when the report is
assembled, by the same code on both paths. The discovery route applied to
each target is in the record for the same reason a measurement is: the run
observed it, the report presents it, and it cannot be established again later
\(em by the time the record is read the tree may no longer be a repository, or
may no longer exist.
.PP
The threshold is therefore
.I not
in the record. One record answers as many threshold questions as you care to
ask:
.PP
.EX
elc \-\-from\-xml build/metrics.xml \-c 10
elc \-\-from\-xml build/metrics.xml \-c 20
.EE
.PP
A record that is not well-formed XML, that does not match
.BR elc 's
own structure, or that carries an unrecognized format version, is
rejected with exit status 2 and no output at all. A partly reconstructed
report is indistinguishable from a complete one once rendered, so none is
attempted.
.PP
Regeneration takes no
.IR TARGET ,
and produces Markdown alone: a saved record carries the findings of a run
rather than the graph, so no companion artefact can be rebuilt from it.
Selecting any other format with
.B \-\-from\-xml
is a usage error rather than a silently ignored request.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
One of
.BR table ,
.BR csv ,
.BR xml ,
or
.BR md ,
for a report written to standard output. The default is
.BR table .
With
.B \-\-output
the filename's extension names the format instead, and this option is
accepted only where it agrees with it. With
.B \-\-from\-xml
the default is
.B md
and no other format is accepted, by either spelling.
.TP
.BI \-\-from\-xml " FILE"
Rebuild a report from a record previously written with
.BR "\-f xml" ,
reading no source file. No
.I TARGET
is given in this mode.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-complexity\-threshold " " \fIN\fR
List a file's functions whose cyclomatic complexity is
.I N
or greater. The default is 15. Listing only: no threshold affects the exit
status.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the report to
.I FILE
instead of to standard output. Diagnostics continue to go to standard error,
so the file holds results and nothing else. The default is standard output.
.IP
The extension of
.I FILE
names the format:
.B .txt
for the aligned table,
.B .md
for Markdown,
.B .csv
for CSV, and
.B .xml
for the complete record. Any other extension, or none, is a usage error
rather than a guess. See
.B FORMATS
above.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Present the verbose report rather than the summary. See
.B REPORT VERBOSITY
below. Presentation only: no measurement, no finding, no severity, and not
the exit status changes with it. The
.B csv
and
.B xml
formats are complete whatever the verbosity, so the option is accepted with
either and has no effect on the file produced.
.TP
.BI \-\-entry " SYMBOL"
Declare
.I SYMBOL
an entry point, from which call depth and reachability are measured.
Repeatable; give one for each. There is no default and none is inferred \(em
.B main
is right for an application, wrong for a library, and badly wrong for firmware
whose interrupt handlers are reached from a vector table and called by nothing.
With none declared, the analyses needing them are omitted with the reason
stated in the report rather than reported as an empty result, and nothing
whatsoever is reported unreachable.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bottleneck\-threshold " " \fIN\fR
Report as an architectural bottleneck every component whose afferent
.I and
efferent coupling are each
.I N
or greater \(em a component simultaneously depended upon widely and dependent
widely, and therefore both dangerous to change and difficult to isolate. The
default is 5. Unlike the published thresholds
.B elc
evaluates against, this one is its own heuristic rather than a standard, and is
reported as such wherever it appears.
Listing only: no threshold affects the exit status.
.TP
.BI \-\-stratum " NAME:GLOB[,GLOB...]"
Declare an architectural layer called
.I NAME
containing the files matching the given shell patterns. Repeatable; naming a
layer again adds patterns to it rather than creating a second one. The order
layers are first declared
.I is
the permitted direction of dependency, topmost first \(em so with
.B app
declared before
.BR hal ,
a call from
.B app
down into
.B hal
is ordinary and one the other way is not. Use
.B \-\-stratum\-order
to state the direction explicitly instead. Strata are never discovered from the
filesystem: a directory layout is a convention, and inferring an architecture
from one would report violations against a design nobody stated.
.TP
.BI \-\-stratum\-order " NAME>NAME[>NAME...]"
State the permitted direction of dependency between the declared layers,
leftmost topmost. May be given before or after the
.B \-\-stratum
declarations it orders. Every declared stratum must appear and every name must
be a declared stratum: a partial order determines no direction, and a name for
a layer that does not exist is a typo whose silent acceptance would leave the
layering validated against something other than what was written.
.TP
.BI \-\-scope " NAME:GLOB[,GLOB...]"
Declare an execution scope called
.I NAME
containing the files matching the given shell patterns. Repeatable; give one
for each scope. Intended for targets whose components share an overlapping
memory map and symbol table \(em a host-driven sequential test harness, or a
bootloader and the application it starts. With two or more declared,
.B elc
reports every call and every shared global object by which one scope reaches a
function or object belonging to another. A file matching no declaration lies
outside the partition rather than in a scope of its own, and edges touching it
are not crossings. With none declared the analysis is omitted with the reason
stated.
.TP
.BI \-D ", " \-\-define " NAME[=VALUE]"
Define a conditional-compilation symbol, so that the metrics describe the
configuration in which it is defined. Repeatable. A condition testing a symbol
no
.B \-D
mentions is
.I undecidable
rather than false: both branches are counted and the region is reported
undecided. With no
.B \-D
at all nothing is pruned on that account. See
.B CONDITIONAL COMPILATION
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-elf " FILE"
Restrict every measurement and every analysis to the functions the linked image
.I FILE
defines, so that the report describes the program the build produced rather
than the source it was drawn from.
.I FILE
is an image \(em an executable, a shared object, or a relocatable object \(em
and not a source tree. It is read for its symbol table alone: no compiler,
linker, or build system is invoked, no other image is searched for, and no
debugging information is required. The source functions the image does not
define are listed, and the linkage names
.B elc
could not decode are counted. With no
.B \-\-elf
nothing is filtered. See
.B FILTERING BY A LINKED IMAGE
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-rules " LANG:PATH"
Check the analysed source against the custom rule query in
.IR PATH ,
compiled for the language
.IR LANG .
Repeatable. A Tree-sitter query compiles against one specific grammar, which is
why the language is named rather than inferred. Rules placed in the runtime
location under
.I queries/LANG/rules/
are used without being named, bound to the language by the directory holding
them; see
.B CUSTOM RULES
below.
.TP
.B \-\-graphml
Also write the System Dependence Graph as GraphML. The file is named from the
report's own output path by extension substitution, so an
.B \-\-output
of
.I report.md
yields
.IR report.graphml ;
the option takes no path of its own. Off unless requested, and with the report
on standard output no file is written at all \(em there is no path to derive a
name from. A companion that cannot be written is a diagnostic and a failed
exit status, never a reason to withhold the report itself.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dot
Do not write the annotated Graphviz call tree. Unlike
.BR \-\-graphml ,
the call tree is written
.I by default
whenever a report goes to a named file, so this switch records a refusal rather
than a request; see
.B THE CALL TREE
below.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display a usage summary on standard output and exit successfully. Requesting
help is not an error, so the exit status is 0 rather than a failure code.
.SS Findings, severity, and attribution
Every analysis above measures. The
.B Findings
section judges, and it is the only part of
.B elc
that does. Each row states what was measured, where, how far outside the
accepted range it fell, and
.I which published source draws that line
\(em MISRA C and its rule number, Robert C. Martin, or the Henry-Kafura
information-flow metrics.
.PP
Exactly one threshold is
.BR elc 's
own rather than a published standard, the bottleneck of
.BR \-\-bottleneck\-threshold ,
and its rows say so where they are read. That label is what lets a reader tell
a standard from a choice.
.PP
The fan-out bands are
.BR exhaustive :
0 to 2 is below the healthy range, 3 to 7 healthy, and 8 to 10 acceptable, none
of which produces a finding; 11 to 15 warns; above 15 is critical. Every value
classifies exactly once.
.PP
A severity is one of
.BR info ", " warning " or " critical ,
exactly one per finding, the highest applicable band winning where two apply.
.B It is a label and nothing more:
a run reporting critical findings still exits 0 provided every file was read,
because the exit status is reserved for failures and deciding what a finding
warrants belongs to the caller. Findings are ranked most severe first, and a
measurement inside its accepted band is still reported in the table that
measured it.
.PP
.B elc
proposes no fix, ranks no design beyond what a cited standard states, and
applies no style opinion of its own.
.SS Partly unparsable files
.B elc
parses source as written and runs no preprocessor, so a grammar occasionally
meets a construct it cannot follow \(em a macro standing in for a string
literal is the usual cause in embedded C. Only the lines it could not read are
lost. The file is measured from the parts the grammar understood, the lines it
could not are counted, and the figure is reported per file and in the project
summary, so a partial measurement is never mistaken for a complete one.
.PP
An unbalanced delimiter is the limit of that recovery: it leaves the parser
nothing to resynchronise on, so everything after it counts as damaged. The line
count covers what was lost, so the scale remains visible. Any file partly
unparsed makes the exit status 1.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The three classes are distinct so that a caller can tell a degraded run from a
run that never happened. No finding severity ever contributes to the exit
status: findings are data, and acting on them is the caller's decision.
.TP
.B 0
Every discovered file was processed without error, or skipped because no
language module was available for it. A skipped file is not a failure.
.TP
.B 1
The run completed and produced a report, but at least one discovered file
failed to be read or parsed. The report covers the files that succeeded.
.TP
.B 2
The run did not complete and no report was produced: a usage error, an
invalid target, a fatal runtime-location failure, or a rejected saved record.
.SH OUTPUT
Results are written to standard output, or to the file named with
.BR \-\-output ;
diagnostics are written to standard error. Nothing else is written to the
results stream, so output may be piped or captured without being corrupted by
diagnostic text.
.PP
The default format is an aligned table in tiers, and by default it presents
the summary tiers alone \(em see
.B REPORT VERBOSITY
below for which those are. Every section a given verbosity reaches is emitted
whether or not it has rows. The example below is of a
.B \-\-verbose
run and is abridged. Column widths are taken from the longest value in each
column so the figures line up:
.PP
.EX
Project summary
  Files               2
  Physical lines     42
  ELOC               18
  Functions           3
  Skipped             1
  Unresolved calls    4

Callouts
  What          Value  Where
  ------------  -----  ---------------------------------
  Largest file     12  /home/u/proj/src/a.c
  Most complex      7  parse in /home/u/proj/src/a.c

Discovery
  Target                Route
  --------------------  ----------
  /home/u/proj/src      repository

Languages
  Language  Files  Lines  ELOC
  --------  -----  -----  ----
  c             2     42    18

Files
  File                  Language  Lines  ELOC  Functions
  --------------------  --------  -----  ----  ---------
  /home/u/proj/src/a.c  c            18    12          2
  /home/u/proj/src/b.c  c            24     6          1

Functions
  File                  Function    Lines  ELOC  Complexity
  --------------------  ----------  -----  ----  ----------
  /home/u/proj/src/a.c  parse        5-19     9           7
  /home/u/proj/src/a.c  emit        21-24     3           1
  /home/u/proj/src/b.c  main         3-11     6           2

At or over the complexity threshold (5)
  File                  Function  Complexity
  --------------------  --------  ----------
  /home/u/proj/src/a.c  parse              7

Skipped files (no language module)
  /home/u/proj/src/notes.md
.EE
.PP
A function's line range runs from its signature to the end of its body. A
named function declared inside another is reported in its own right rather
than folded into the one containing it.
.PP
A file's ELOC may exceed the sum of its functions': a statement outside every
function — an initialised global, say — counts for the file and for no
function. It may also fall below that sum, where two functions share a line.
.PP
Every section is emitted whether or not it has rows, so the report has the
same shape whatever the target held. Files are listed by canonical absolute
path in ascending byte order, and functions within a file in start-line
order. That
order is a property of the report rather than of the filesystem, so two runs
over the same tree produce byte-identical output, and so do two runs that
name the same targets in a different order.
.PP
A run in which no file was found still produces this report, with zero totals
and an empty table, and exits successfully. Silence would be
indistinguishable from a failure.
.PP
The shape of the report does not vary with the type of the target: a single
file, a plain directory, and a repository all produce the same headings and
the same columns.
.SH REPORT VERBOSITY
A report is presented at one of two verbosities. The default is the
.IR summary ,
which is what
.B elc
prints when
.B \-\-verbose
is not given;
.B \-\-verbose
selects the
.IR verbose
report, which is every tier the summary presents together with the detail
tiers it omits.
.PP
The partition is by tier and is fixed, so that it is a property of the report
rather than of how a renderer happened to be written. A tier presenting a
project\-level aggregate, a file's own totals, or a finding you are expected
to act on is a summary tier. A tier enumerating one row per analysed entity
\(em per function, per global object, per unreachable statement, per graph
edge, per custom\-rule match \(em is a detail tier.
.PP
The summary tiers, in the order they are presented:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Project summary, and the project\-wide callouts.
.IP \(bu 2
Discovery \(em the route applied to each directory target.
.IP \(bu 2
Languages \(em the totals broken down by language.
.IP \(bu 2
Files \(em each file's own totals.
.IP \(bu 2
At or over the complexity threshold \(em the per\-file threshold listing.
.IP \(bu 2
Any analysis omitted for want of a declaration, with its reason. The section
carrying the notice is a detail tier, but the notice itself is not: an
analysis nobody declared for is something you must be told at either
verbosity.
.IP \(bu 2
Findings \(em every measurement that crossed a published line, ranked by
severity. The summary keeps these deliberately: a summary that dropped the
one section you act on would be shorter and useless.
.IP \(bu 2
Conditional\-compilation definitions \(em the configuration in force.
.IP \(bu 2
Linked\-image filter \(em the image the run was filtered by, where one was
named.
.IP \(bu 2
Partially parsed files.
.IP \(bu 2
Skipped files.
.RE
.PP
The detail tiers, presented only with
.BR \-\-verbose :
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Functions \(em one row per analysed function.
.IP \(bu 2
Fan\-out, recursion, the deepest call chain, component coupling, component
dependency cycles, and layering \(em the measurements taken over the
dependence graph, one row per entity. Every one of these that crossed a
published line is a finding, and the findings are in the summary.
.IP \(bu 2
Global state, and the globals reachable from nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
Unreachable functions, and dead code within functions.
.IP \(bu 2
Cross\-scope access.
.IP \(bu 2
The functions a linked image does not define.
.IP \(bu 2
Custom rule matches.
.RE
.PP
The verbose report is exactly what
.B elc
presented before the summary became the default: the option is a restoration
rather than a new format to learn.
.PP
Verbosity governs presentation and nothing else. It changes no measurement,
no finding, no severity, and not the exit status; a value absent from a
summary is absent because it was not printed rather than because it was not
computed. The
.B csv
and
.B xml
formats are defined as complete \(em one record per function, and every
element of a run \(em so there is no presentation for a verbosity to select
between. Both are byte\-identical at either verbosity, and asking for
.B \-\-verbose
with either is accepted rather than rejected: nothing about the request is
contradictory, and it simply has no effect.
.PP
Because the record is complete whatever the verbosity, one saved record
answers a summary question and a verbose one alike, and answers each
identically to a direct run:
.PP
.EX
elc \-f xml \-o build/metrics.xml src/
elc \-\-from\-xml build/metrics.xml            # summary Markdown
elc \-\-from\-xml build/metrics.xml \-\-verbose  # verbose Markdown
.EE
.SH CONDITIONAL COMPILATION
Source that is conditionally compiled describes several programs, and measuring
it without saying which one produces the union of them all \(em a figure that
describes no build that exists. Naming a configuration with
.B \-D
measures that configuration instead.
.PP
.EX
elc -DFEATURE_X -DTARGET=stm32 src/
.EE
.PP
.B elc
runs no preprocessor. It does not invoke
.BR cpp ,
a compiler, or a build system, and it reads no file the source includes; a
result that depended on which toolchain was installed would not be a property
of the source. What it does instead is decide each region from the syntax tree
it already parsed.
.SS What is decided, and what is not
A region is decided in exactly two cases:
.TP
.B a constant condition
.B #if 0
and
.B #if 1
are the same in every configuration, so they are pruned whether or not any
.B \-D
was given.
.TP
.B a definedness test over a symbol you named
.BR #ifdef ", " #ifndef ", and " "#if defined(...)"
are decided when the symbol appears in a
.BR \-D .
.PP
Everything else is
.B undecidable rather than false.
.B #if VERSION > 2
needs macro values
.B elc
does not have; both branches stay counted and the region is added to the
.B Undecided regions
figure in the project summary.
.PP
That asymmetry is deliberate and is the whole safety argument. Treating an
unrecognised condition as false would silently delete code and produce a report
that is confidently wrong and looks exactly like a correct one. Treating it as
true over-counts, which is visible in a figure printed beside the metrics.
.SS A symbol you did not name is not thereby undefined
.B \-D
can only assert that a symbol
.I is
defined; there is no
.BR \-U .
A symbol you never mention may still be defined by a header or by a build
system
.B elc
never sees, so such a condition is undecidable rather than false.
.PP
It follows that supplying no definitions prunes nothing on that account, and
that an
.B #ifndef
include guard is undecidable in every run. A header-heavy project therefore
reports a large undecided count, which is honest rather than a defect.
.SS Which constructs are conditional is runtime data
The constructs that introduce a region, where the condition sits, and which
part is the alternative are declared by the language's
.I conditionals.scm
query file, not by
.BR elc .
A C preprocessor conditional and a Rust
.B #[cfg]
attribute are the same mechanism, and a language whose module supplies no such
file simply has no conditional compilation. See
.I runtime/queries/README.md
in the distribution.
.PP
An attribute has no
.BR #else ,
so a Rust
.B #[cfg(X)]
item can only be removed and never swapped for another. It is
.B #[cfg(not(X))]
with
.BI \-D X
that prunes.
.SS The configuration is reported and recorded
The definitions in force appear in the report and in the saved record, so a
report states which configuration it describes and a regenerated one still
does. Because pruning happens when a file is measured rather than when a report
is rendered, combining
.B \-D
with
.B \-\-from\-xml
is a usage error rather than a silently ignored request.
.SH FILTERING BY A LINKED IMAGE
Source holds functions a build does not keep: excluded by configuration,
discarded by the linker as unreachable, or belonging to a translation unit the
link never included. A report covering them describes no image that exists and
overstates every measure taken from it. Naming the image with
.B \-\-elf
measures what shipped.
.PP
.EX
elc \-\-elf build/app.elf src/
.EE
.PP
This is the question of
.B CONDITIONAL COMPILATION
answered a different way, and neither answer replaces the other. A
.B \-D
re\-decides the conditions a build resolved, from definitions you restate; an
image was produced by the real toolchain with the real flags and
.I observes what the build did.
Where both are available the image is the stronger evidence \(em but it says
only which
.I functions
survived, and nothing about which lines inside one were compiled out. The two
compose, and may be given together.
.SS What counts as a function the image defines
A symbol is taken as one when it is of function type
.I and
is defined by the image rather than imported by it. Both halves matter: without
the type test an object and a function of the same name are indistinguishable,
and without the definedness test every function the image
.I calls
out to a shared library would count as one the image contains \(em and the
filter would then retain source the build never compiled.
.PP
.B elc
reads
.I .symtab
where the image has one and
.I .dynsym
where it does not. A stripped image has neither, and is an error rather than an
empty filter; see
.B An unusable image is an error
below. An image reduced to its dynamic table yields a smaller set, since that
table holds only the exported subset, and a correspondingly longer list of
functions it does not define.
.SS Names that reach the image encoded
C is the only supported language whose linkage name is its source name. A C++
or Rust function is recorded under a mangled name, and matching those raw would
retain nothing at all.
.B elc
decodes the Itanium C++ ABI \(em which covers C++ and Rust's legacy scheme \(em
and reduces the result to the identifier the report presents:
.B _ZNK2ns1C1fEi
decodes to
.B ns::C::f(int) const
and matches a source function named
.BR f .
The scheme is detected from the name rather than from a language you state,
since an image may hold symbols from several compilers.
.PP
A name encoded by a scheme this build does not decode \(em Rust's v0 mangling,
where the C++ runtime is not new enough for it \(em resolves to nothing and is
.I counted,
not silently dropped. The count appears as
.B Unresolved linkage names
in the report, so a language whose compiler uses such a scheme reports a large
count and says so rather than appearing to work.
.SS What the filter reports
Two figures, and they are different claims.
.TP
.B Unresolved linkage names
How completely the image could be read: the claim the
.B Unresolved calls
figure makes about the graph, and for the same reason \(em a filter whose
accuracy is unstated cannot be acted on.
.TP
.B Functions the image does not define
The finding the option exists to produce. This is dead code established by what
the linker did, rather than inferred from the call graph as the
.B Unreachable functions
section is, so the two are reported separately and neither is presented as the
other.
.PP
Effective lines of code belonging to no function \(em an initialised object at
file scope, say \(em are
.I retained,
and reported as
.B ELOC outside any function.
The image's function set says nothing about code that is not a function, so
excluding it would be a claim
.B elc
cannot support, and folding it into the totals would hide the one part of them
the filter did not narrow.
.PP
A call whose target the filter removed is counted
.I unresolved
rather than resolved to a function that is not in the image, so the
reachability analysis stays sound.
.SS An unusable image is an error
An image that is absent, unreadable, not an object file, of a class this build
does not read, or carrying no function symbols at all ends the run with a
diagnostic naming it and no report, before any source file is measured. You
named the file, so the failure is yours to correct.
.PP
The fully stripped image is the case that most needs this. An empty function
set would otherwise filter every function away and report a project containing
none \(em a confidently wrong result that no reader could tell from a correct
one.
.SS The filter is reported and recorded
The image and both counts appear in the report and in the saved record, so a
report states which image it describes and a regenerated one still does.
Because the filter is applied when a file is measured rather than when a report
is rendered, combining
.B \-\-elf
with
.B \-\-from\-xml
is a usage error rather than a silently ignored request.
.SH CUSTOM RULES
A custom rule is a Tree-sitter query you write, checked against the analysed
source by the same mechanism that produces
.BR elc 's
own metrics \(em during the same parse, with the same predicate handling. A
file of them needs no rebuild and no change to
.BR elc :
it is data.
.PP
.B elc
reports what a rule matched and forms no opinion about it. A match carries no
severity and cites no source, because there is nothing honest to put in
either: it was your decision that the rule was worth writing, not
.BR elc 's.
Matches appear in a section of their own beside the
.B Findings
and never among them.
.SS Identity
A rule's identity is the basename of the file containing it together with the
capture name that matched, so one file expresses as many independently named
rules as it holds captures. A
.I house-style.scm
capturing
.B @allocation
and
.B @jump
yields
.I house-style.allocation
and
.IR house-style.jump .
The capture name is therefore user-visible in a way it is not elsewhere; name
it for whoever reads the report.
.SS Where a rule may come from
Two places, and no others:
.TP
.I <runtime>/queries/<lang>/rules/*.scm
Bound to the language by the directory holding it, and used without being
named.
.TP
.BI \-\-rules " lang:path"
Bound by the argument.
.PP
.B No rule file is ever discovered from the working directory, the analysis
.BR target, " or a dotfile."
Two people running the same command on the same tree must obtain the same
result, and a rule picked up from a checkout would make that false. This is the
same guarantee that admits no configuration file; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.SS When a rule is broken
Where the file came from decides what happens, not what is wrong with it. A
rule you named on the command line is a mistake you can correct now:
.B elc
diagnoses it and stops before analysing any file. A rule sitting in the runtime
location is a malformed component and is handled like any other \(em
diagnosed, excluded, and the run continues. A rule naming a language for which
no module is available is reported and skipped from either place, since what is
missing is the module rather than the rule.
.SH THE CALL TREE
Alongside a report written to a named file,
.B elc
writes the call tree as a Graphviz
.I .dot
file, annotated with the architectural findings that apply to it. The file is
named from the report's own output path by extension substitution, so an
.B \-\-output
of
.I report.md
yields
.IR report.dot ;
it takes no path of its own.
.PP
.B elc
writes the file and renders nothing. Graphviz is a separate tool the user may
run on the result, and
.B elc
neither links it nor invokes it:
.PP
.EX
elc --entry main -o report.md src/
dot -Tsvg report.dot -o report.svg
.EE
.PP
Generation is enabled by default and is declined with
.BR \-\-no\-dot .
With the report on standard output no file is written whether or not
generation was declined \(em there is no output path to derive a name from. A
file that cannot be written is a diagnostic and a failed exit status, never a
reason to withhold the report itself.
.SS What the drawing shows
Each source file becomes a labelled cluster and each function a node within
it; the edges are calls. Coupling through a shared global object is
.I not
drawn as an edge, because it is not a call \(em it appears instead as a
property of the functions that take part in it.
.PP
Every annotation is a Graphviz attribute a renderer is free to ignore. Ignore
all of them and the same tree remains, with the same nodes and the same edges,
undecorated:
.TP
.B filled amber or red
A warning- or critical-severity finding applies to the node. The severity is
the one the
.B Findings
section reports, on the authority named there; the drawing colours it and
decides nothing.
.TP
.B double border
A member of a recursive cycle. Every member is marked, not only the one the
finding is filed against.
.TP
.B octagon
Takes part in a hidden channel \(em a global object shared across regions of
the call graph that never call each other.
.TP
.B note shape
The only function naming some global object, which is therefore a candidate
for a narrower scope.
.TP
.B dashed grey
No path reaches the function from any declared entry point. Requires
.BR \-\-entry ;
without it the analysis is omitted and nothing is dashed.
.TP
.B thick blue
A step of the deepest call chain, on the nodes and on the edges between them.
.TP
.B coloured cluster
A finding applies to the source file as a whole \(em a dependency cycle with
another file, an architectural bottleneck, or an instability that contradicts
its declared layer. Every member of a cycle is coloured, not only the file the
finding is filed against.
.PP
Each node's tooltip carries its definition site and its findings in full, and
the head of the file carries the same key in a comment. Both survive being
ignored.
.PP
Two functions in different files that share a name are indistinguishable to
the graph, which resolves calls by name alone, and the drawing inherits every
consequence of that. See the caveat under
.B Call-tree analyses
in
.BR DESCRIPTION ,
and read the diagnostics on standard error before circulating a drawing.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ELC_RUNTIME_DIR
Location of the runtime directory holding language grammars and their query
files. When set, it takes precedence over every path derived from the
executable, and is used exactly as given: a value naming a directory that is
not there is reported against that path rather than falling back to one the
user did not choose.
.IP
When unset, two paths relative to the executable are tried in order and the
first that exists is used \(em
.I runtime
beside the binary, then
.I ../share/elc/runtime
\(em so an
.B elc
installed to
.I /usr/local/bin
finds its runtime at
.IR /usr/local/share/elc/runtime .
Where neither exists the diagnostic names both.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <prefix>/share/elc/runtime/
The runtime language-support directory of an installed
.BR elc ,
and the second of the two paths tried relative to the executable. The first is
.I runtime/
beside the binary, which serves an unpacked self-contained copy and the build
tree.
.B ELC_RUNTIME_DIR
overrides both. The paths below are relative to whichever of these is in use.
.TP
.I runtime/parsers/<lang>.so
Tree-sitter grammar for a language, loaded on first use.
.TP
.I runtime/queries/<lang>/
The query files that define what elc extracts for that language.
.TP
.I runtime/extensions.map
Maps a file extension to a language name, one pair per line. Editing this
file changes what is detected; no rebuild is needed.
.TP
.I runtime/queries/README.md
The published contract a language module must satisfy: the query files it
supplies and the capture names by which they return their results.
.TP
.I runtime/binary.exts
Extensions excluded from the directory walk, one per line. Editing this file
changes what is excluded; no rebuild is needed.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B elc
has none. Behaviour is determined entirely by the command line and the
contents of the runtime directory. No configuration file or dotfile is read
or discovered, from the working directory, the analysis target, or any
ancestor of either, so two users running the same command on the same tree
obtain the same result.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B elc \-\-help
Show the usage summary.
.TP
.B elc src/
Analyse a directory.
.TP
.B elc src/main.c src/cli.c
Analyse named files.
.TP
.B elc src/main.c src/
Analyse a file and a directory that contains it. The file is reported once.
.TP
.B elc \-o report.txt src/
Write the report to a file, leaving standard output empty. The
.B .txt
names the aligned table; no
.B \-f
is needed.
.TP
.B elc \-o report.csv src/
Write CSV, with no format option given: the filename has already said so.
.TP
.B elc \-\-verbose src/
Present every tier, including the per\-function and per\-entity tables the
summary omits.
.TP
.B elc src/ lib/ tools/
Analyse a target holding several languages at once.
.TP
.B elc \-c 10 src/
List every function whose complexity is 10 or greater. The exit status is
unchanged by what the list contains.
.TP
.B elc \-f md src/ > report.md
Produce a Markdown report.
.TP
.B elc \-f csv src/ | cut \-d, \-f3,7
Take the function names and their complexities into another tool.
.TP
.B elc \-f xml \-o run.xml src/ && elc \-\-from\-xml run.xml \-c 8
Keep a record of a run, then ask it a question the original run did not.
.TP
.B elc \-\-from\-xml run.xml \-\-verbose
Regenerate the same record in full. Verbosity is a property of the report
rather than of the record, so one record answers both.
.TP
.B ELC_RUNTIME_DIR=/opt/elc/runtime elc src/
Analyse with language support taken from a runtime directory elsewhere.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cloc (1),
.BR gprof (1)
.PP
